														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2349806">
									

<p>Ok, here's my take on sexual identity and orientation as far as fiction is concerned:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Show&nbsp;rant&nbsp;</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Hide&nbsp;Rant&nbsp;</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>In a situation where the sexual orientation or identity of the characters provides context that changes if the orientations or identities change, then write what conveys the intention best. An example? The film &quot;Brokeback Mountain.&quot; (Note: I've never seen the film, only heard and read plot summaries and the like.) If you switched one of the two characters to a woman and made the relationship cis-het, it literally becomes meaningless. The emotional impact of that film came from the forbidden love angle, which came from two manly men who were also gay and in love with each other. Another, similar example is the interracial relationship in the novel (and film adaptation) &quot;Holes.&quot;</p>
<p>Here, the emotional impact comes from a misunderstanding in the delivery of a letter, and a tragedy that follows. Making a character here gay or trans or an extraterrestrial or a talking mushroom doesn't matter, and, even if gay people are underrepresented in media, this story isn't a situation wherein arms should be taken up over it.</p>
<p>Would making this story about gay people add the forbidden love angle, or make the characters more relatable to the gay folks who read this site? Perhaps, but I don't think it's an offense, or really necessary. Writing a heterosexual relationship isn't homophobic; bashing gay people or ignoring them is, and this story doesn't do that.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Hide&nbsp;Rant&nbsp;</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Tl;dr: I don't think it's being offensive and don't see the source of offense, and I think I've taken the needs of the people claiming offense into account.</p>
<p>Edit: let me elaborate a little bit more. If the author had originally written this as gay, I would be fine with that. There's nothing wrong with gay romantic tragedy wherein the tragedy doesn't come from the sexual orientation of the characters. I just don't think the author should feel a need to change something that isn't actively offensive.</p>

							</div>

								
