				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thedeadlymoose" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(732274); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=732274&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606360693" alt="thedeadlymoose" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=732274)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thedeadlymoose" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(732274); return false;" >thedeadlymoose</a></span> <span class="odate time_1438407701 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Aug 2015 05:41</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2349854">
									

<p>I wasn't actually going to say anything about this beyond maybe a joke comment (I gave Cimmerian extensive feedback elsewhere, and it wasn't exactly 'make it gayer') but this argument is starkly off-base.</p>
<p>(Although, as an aside, I have to admit I think the comments about making it gayer are incredibly funny to me, given that those comments usually only appear on pieces about queer characters demanding that they be made straighter. Though I know Cimmerian is understandably confused.)</p>
<p>Anyway, response under the cut.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The <span style="text-decoration: underline;">general</span> issue (not specific to this article; no raindrop feels responsible for the flood, but still) is that defaulting to straight/cis/white/male/etc people in every story is that it's a cliche, often dull, and often artistically inferior, aside from being socially negative.</p>
<p>Your argument has the implication that you shouldn't include queer or interracial relationships without a special plot reason for it, which is usually how things are, and also why queer and interracial relationships barely exist in media, unlike real life. This is a problem. Limiting our existence to 'issue stories' is a problem.</p>
<p>Also, the story <em>does</em> ignore gay people. Since there's only two people there, and it's irrelevant to the story, it's not necessarily bad, but it is <em>literally true</em>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Making a character here gay or trans or an extraterrestrial or a talking mushroom doesn't matter, and, even if gay people are underrepresented in media, this story isn't a situation wherein arms should be taken up over it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes it does. Doing any of these things would make the story more interesting, even if by only a little, while also bringing other effects into the story (such as comedy, etc). This is what happens every time you move away from the default, from what the reader is trained to expect.</p>
<p>It's also an especially weird argument because making them talking mushrooms would make the entire piece obviously, drastically, dramatically different, and I imagine if you reflect on that, you'll agree.</p>
<p>(gay&#8230;&#8230;. alien&#8230;&#8230;&#8230;. mushrooms&#8230;&#8230;&#8230;&#8230;..)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and I think I've taken the needs of the people claiming offense into account.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No one's claimed offense. They've claimed boredom, and were being silly about it in tone.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Alright. Now for my own feedback, also under a cut.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I already gave Cimmerian feedback elsewhere, but here we go: I don't find this very compelling. It's certainly alright, but it reads as boring to me.</p>
<p>A note on the letter, which others will disagree with. The letter wasn't compelling enough to me &#8212; I watched a let's play of Silent Hill 2's In Water ending, which had a really heartbreaking letter, and which I couldn't help but compare this unfavorably to. Though it isn't <em>bad</em>, and it's the thing I'd change last if I were trying to improve this.</p>
<p>Yes, making the characters queer would make it more interesting, but not by very much; it wouldn't fix the other issues. I'd upvote if that change were made, but that's for personal reasons (it would add just enough extra resonance). I'm an easy upvote (really). It wouldn't alone actually fix the issues I perceive.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>tl;dr, I would wager that if this work were sufficiently interesting, no one would think to question the generic heterosexuality.</p>
<p>(even though everything should be gay, PANSEXUAL NONBINARY DREADLORDS FOREVER)</p>
<p>EDIT: Someone asked why I would upvote for the reason of a queer character in this case. I'll copy my [edited] conversation with Cimmerian to explain why.</p>
<p>Personal opinions included. Click with care.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>&lt;thedeadlymoose&gt; But yeah, to give a quicker answer to your earlier question, changing races/genders/etc away from the default is 'better' just in the same sense that changing any detail from a default is 'better'. The reader always wants to see what's unexpected, as a general rule<br />
&lt;thedeadlymoose&gt; That's the only sense it's meant in. (Obviously, 'default' people are not better than 'non-default' people or traits.)<br />
&lt;thedeadlymoose&gt; It wouldn't actually fix the issues I perceive<br />
&lt;Cimmerian&gt; I get what you're saying. It would still have the same problems but it would resonate more with you?<br />
&lt;thedeadlymoose&gt; Yes. I would upvote largely for that reason.<br />
&lt;thedeadlymoose&gt; I'm an easy upvote, frankly.<br />
&lt;thedeadlymoose&gt; But artistically speaking, you ought to do more. And the comments identifying that as the Real Problem are, frankly, vapid. In my unofficial opinion.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
</div>
</div>

