<p>My reply still stands with the edit.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I just don't think this is offensive</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Neither does anyone else, as I said.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and don't think making it gay would make it more interesting.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I explained this already. But the rest of my analysis will go under a cut.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Literally <em>any</em> detail that's less expected makes something more interesting. This is commonly done on the character level by adding unusual background details, atypical professions, surprising personality quirks, bodily scars (abused by cinema until a single scar across an eye became just another cliche).</p>
<p>Sexual orientation of characters is not an exception to this rule. When almost every character is X, then making a character Y is more interesting by default.</p>
<p>If it doesn't interest you personally, that's fine, and perfectly reasonable, but I'm talking general principle here, because there's literally zero point trying to debate personal taste in this case.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I also don't think the author should feel inclined to change a work when they did nothing <em>wrong.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>That is not how editing works. You don't need to do something <em>wrong</em> to have a reason to change it. In fact, in professional editing, it often amounts to replacing one perfectly fine thing with another perfectly fine thing, just to keep your editor happy, but I don't mean that here.</p>
<p>I mean that if you only improve things by changing the things that are <em>actually wrong</em>, then you won't get very far. The best writers don't settle for merely <em>not wrong</em>.</p>
<p>Look up earlier drafts of masterpiece works sometime to see what I mean. Or even just shooting script vs final script. This can be&#8230; illuminating. My favorite, easy-to-find example is the initial pilot of BBC Sherlock, versus the final pilot. Both are very good. The second is drastically better.</p>
<p>EDIT: For clarity, this is about good writing principle. It does not necessarily apply to this particular change to this particular story.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
